  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThe central idea is to develop a structured optimization method that improves upon the current local search techniques by combining global exploration with targeted refinement. This approach is motivated by the observation that:\n- The current method's iterative refinement risks convergence to local minima\n- Noisy gradient information dominates the search space exploration\n- Direct resolution of the non-convex constraints is computationally prohibitive\n\nThe proposed method introduces four distinct optimization phases with increasing computational intensity, designed to systematically explore the landscape of feasible solutions. This phase-based structure allows prioritizing promising regions while maintaining feasibility guarantees throughout the optimization process.\n\nThe key innovation is the appearance and persistence of specialized operators across optimization phases:\n- Ascent operators to systematically increase solution quality through correlation scoring\n- Following operators that refine promising patterns found by ascent methods\n- Proximity operators that construct solutions from high-quality neighbors\n- These operators form a foundation for material improvements over the current heuristic approaches\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe optimization is formulated as:\nMinimize: C_5 = max_k (dx) * \u222bh(x)(1-h(x+k))dx\nSubject to:\n0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n\u2211h[i] = n/2\ndx = 2.0/n\n\nThe separation of optimization into distinct phases enables:\n- Gradient-based exploration in the final phase\n- Early-stage operators that work with normalized correlation scores\n- Nonlinear transformations that maintain integrality constraints\n- The core innovation lies in operators that explicitly maximize correlation minimum values\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\nThe implementation follows six core stages:\n\nStage 1: Initialization (n=300)\n- Start with either provided initial_h_values or uniform random solution\n- Project to satisfy constraints via iterative clipping\n- Compute initial C_5 score\n\nStage 2: Ascent Phase (Budget: 33%)\n- Apply gradient-free ascent using normalized correlation scoring\n- Maximize E_\u03c4 = \u2211 (\u03c4 - \u03c1) where \u03c4 is time budget parameter\n- Uses constraint-penalty formulation E = (1/n) \u2211 (h_i - d)^2 + (1/\u03c3) (log(1 + |max(correlation)|))\n- C_\u03c4 = (2.0/n) * min_k (overlap + boundary term)\n\nStage 3: Following Phase (Budget: 25%)\n- Select high-scoring patterns from Ascent results\n- Apply refined minimization targeting candidate E = \u03b4\u03f5 - \u03b7|sum(h) - n/2|\n- Uses GKX algorithm with adaptive penalty (\u03a3 = 5 * initial \u03a3)\n\nStage 4: Mixed-Phase (Phase Ph): (Budget: 10% divided across phases)\n- For each lag aperture:\n  - Apply specialized operators maintaining L2-norm projection\n  - Includes box-constrained minimization with trust regions\n  - Certification against \u221e-norm violations\n\nStage 5: Sampling/Pruning (Budget: 15%)\n- Maintain valid parent solutions via resampling + projection\n- Prune dominated candidates using custom dominance criteria: \n  Primary: lower C_5 score\n  Secondary: lower transition entropy\n- Dynamic grid parameterization based on convergence rate\n\nStage 6: Final Optimization (Budget: 10%)\n- Convert top performers to continuous space for quasi-Newton optimization\n- Use dual-form equality constraints\n- L1-penalized projected Newton method\n- Returns candidate with minimal C_5 (2.0/n * max_lag_complement)\n\n4. Search Choices and Useful Variations\nDefault configuration:\n- Core operators: Ascent+Eigensolver+Projection\n- \u03c6_penalty = 5e-3\n- \u03c6_adapt = 0.95\n- num_ascent_steps = 300\n\nAdaptive variations:\n- Time-dependent penalty strength: \u03c6_decay(t) = \u03c6_0 * exp(-\u03b2t)\n- Dynamic lag weighting: \u03c9_k = e^(-0.1k) / sum_e^(-0.1j)\n- Grid point reuse: f_reuse = 0.8 between phases\n- Lag emphasis: initial full lag search then focus on top 20 lags\n\nStrategic Accelerations:\n1. Early termination: assess convergence rate at 25% budget mark\n2. Convergence diagnostics: analyze C_5 reduction trajectory\n3. Operator chaining: success of Ascent -> double repetition count\n\n5. Compute Budget and Robustness\nTime allocation:\n- Ascent Phase: ~550 evaluations (33%)\n- Following Phase: 350 evaluations (25%)\n- Mixed-Phase: 200 evaluations (10%)\n- Sampling Phase: 150 evaluations (15%)\n- Final Phase: 100 evaluations (10%)\n\nTermination triggers:\n- Time budget exceeded\n- Convergence rate drop by factor 0.8\n- p-value test for score stagnation\n\n6. Validation and Return Contract\nFinal candidate validation:\n- Projection ensures divisible integrals\n- Resampling maintains valid summation\n- C_5 recomputation via full correlation\n- Dominance ordering verified\n- Performance bounds certified by MISE values",
